 ".claude/rules/learnings/pr.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-pr-reviewer-fold.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/change-evidence-design.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/pr-reviewer-fold.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Removing learnings-files --for-diff breaks an owner-ruled requirement that depends on it (#928 D-7), and nothing records the conflict", "recommendation": "documentation/issues/928-requirements.md (status: draft, D-1 to D-12 owner-ruled) says in D-7 and table rows 26-27 that \"Area learnings load on both hosts through the explicit `learnings-files --for-diff` read\" and that \"the Critic [is required] to read its result\". This branch deletes that flag (plugin/bin/prawduct-hook cmd_learnings_files, gates.learnings_review_change_set, learnings_files.files_for_paths). The 2026-11-10 and 2026-11-11 rulings that remove it, the plan's governed_by and the change record never mention #928, so the Codex adapter work will be designed against a command that no longer exists, and its owner ruling now has no mechanism behind it. Scope: class. It broke because Chunk 04's falsifying search treated documentation/issues/ as history, but a draft requirements doc states live requirements. Search live requirement and design docs for each retired name (`for-diff`, `affecting`, `created-since`, `pr-review-payload`): this also finds documentation/issues/826-requirements.md:140 and route (b) of .prawduct/artifacts/mcp-knowledge-corpus-design.md (\"The Critic already computes its learnings read-set via `learnings-files --for-diff`\"). For each one, either record beneath the ruling that its mechanism was removed and the question is reopened (put D-7 back to the owner, since a ruling is not amended to match code), or descope it explicitly. Do not restore the flag silently.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/learnings_files.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "gates.session_changes_all_non_judgeable lost its only production caller, and two descriptions still name it as a live Stop-hook carveout", "recommendation": "cmd_stop computed `doc_only` from gates.session_changes_all_non_judgeable, and only Gate 3 (the deleted PR-evidence gate) read it. This branch removes both, so the function now has no caller outside tests: tests/test_session_critic_gate.py still pins it at five sites. Meanwhile coverage.check_pr_doc_only's docstring still says the PR skill's skip is \"mirroring the session-end stop-hook carveout (`gates.session_changes_all_non_judgeable`)\", and tests/test_critic_gate_fallthrough.py's header comment calls it the Stop gate's \"other carveout\". The plan's own mid-build decision says functions go with the operations that were their only callers (learnings_change_set, files_for_paths and affected_matches did), and this one was missed. A maintainer who trusts either description will keep the dead predicate in sync with the Stop hook, which no longer reads it. Scope: instance. Delete the function and its tests, then delete the two sentences, or point them at whatever the Stop hook's Critic gate actually keys on (`_judgeable_work`).", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The rewritten /prawduct:pr flow has no verification beyond tests, and the live run the plan says is 'queued' has no durable queue", "recommendation": "The plan's Level and Verification Strategy say a live `/prawduct:pr create` through the new flow \"is queued for the first fresh session after the installed plugin advances\". No operator-verification entry, backlog item or change-record line holds that queue. The only mention is the gitignored session handoff, which says nothing about this run. The unexercised steps include the harness-granted `--body-file .prawduct/.pr-body.md` and `git clean -fx` sequence, check-pr-body against a real template, and the stale-session refusal path. Because nothing holds the queue, the first real PR will run them with nobody watching for the result. Scope: instance. File the live run where something will surface it (a backlog item citing #1129, or an operator-verification entry), and cite it from the plan's Level line. Keep the 'queued' claim only once that home exists.", "files": ["plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Removing Stop-hook Gate 3 left the porcelain doc-only predicate with no production caller, still cited as the live stop-hook carveout", "recommendation": "Why it broke: excising Gate 3 deleted the only production use of `doc_only = gates.session_changes_all_non_judgeable(...)` in `cmd_stop`, and nobody checked what that gate had called (hook-surface rule 'Excising a subsystem silently kills incidental work it happened to host \u2014 audit what IT called'). The result is that `gates.session_changes_all_non_judgeable` is dead code, kept alive only by tests/test_session_critic_gate.py. Three pieces of prose still present it as live. (1) `coverage.check_pr_doc_only`'s docstring says Step 1b mirrors 'the session-end stop-hook carveout (`gates.session_changes_all_non_judgeable`)', but the stop hook's carveout is now `session_work_span(...)['judgeable']`. (2) `gitstate.py`'s 'moved to lib/gates.py as session_changes_all_non_judgeable' comment. (3) The `cmd_stop` comment 'The two must move together or not at all', which now pairs the span with a porcelain predicate that is no longer computed. Consequence: a maintainer changing the doc-only carveout edits or tests the wrong predicate, and the test suite stays green for a function no gate reads. Scope: class, bounded to what Gate 3 alone reached. Search `cmd_stop`'s removed block for its callees; `_pr_diff_is_doc_only` is still live through `check-pr-doc-only`. Remedy: delete `session_changes_all_non_judgeable` and its tests, or deliberately repoint them, and fix the docstring so it names `session_work_span`. Delete the gitstate comment and the 'two must move together' sentence.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "PR skill Step 3's exit-3 bullet invites carrying an unreadable credential check into the description, which the code fails closed on", "recommendation": "Framework Check 8 (Instruction Clarity). `pr_gate.hygiene_exit` returns 3 in two cases: when the run could not answer at all, and when a REFUSING result is `unchecked` (the credential-shape diff could not be read). In the second case `report.error` is None, so stdout prints the normal header plus 'credential-shape: unchecked \u2014 ...', and stderr carries nothing. SKILL.md's Exit 3 bullet describes exit 3 only as 'could not answer at all (an unresolvable base, unreadable git)'. It then says, in the same bullet: 'A single result it could not read prints as `unchecked` ... on an otherwise-answered run: that result is not clean, so carry it into the description as unchecked.' A model looking at an exit 3 whose output is otherwise answered and shows one unchecked result matches the second sentence and proceeds to open the PR, skipping the one control this gate exists to enforce. api-contract.md states the rule correctly ('an unreadable refusing check is 3'); the skill does not. Scope: instance. Remedy: state relationally in the exit-3 bullet that exit 3 includes an unchecked refusing result (credential-shape) and never proceeds. Move the 'carry it into the description' sentence to the exit-0 path, where unchecked warnings and information results actually occur. Pin both with a test in tests/test_pr_skill.py.", "files": ["plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "PR skill Step 1b describes the doc-only predicate as 'ends in .md', contradicting Step 1c's note and the code", "recommendation": "Framework Check 9 (sections contradict each other). This change edited Step 1b's sentence, which still says the fast path applies 'when every file in merge-base...HEAD ends in `.md` and none is governance-protected'. Step 1c's note says the opposite: 'Judgeable is the same predicate Step 1b uses (`coverage_algebra.is_judgeable_path`), not \"is it `.md`\"'. `check_pr_doc_only` also refuses on shipped suite-coupled state, which the sentence omits. Consequence: an agent predicting or explaining the skip (Step 1b tells it to tell the user 'which gate was skipped and why') gives the wrong reason, both for a `.prawduct/`-only branch and for one carrying suite-coupled state. Scope: instance. Remedy: make the sentence relational. Say the fast path applies when `check-pr-doc-only` finds no judgeable file (the predicate Step 1c's note names) and no suite-coupled state. Do not reword it into a second enumeration.", "files": ["plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The PR skill writes a new per-clone scratch file, .prawduct/.pr-body.md, that is in no session-file registry site and is removed only on the happy path", "recommendation": "Create Step 3 and Update Step 3 of plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md write `.prawduct/.pr-body.md` and delete it with `git clean -fx` only after `gh pr create`/`gh pr edit` succeeds. Several things can stop the flow before that: a check-pr-body exit-1 loop the user abandons, a `gh` failure, or a crashed session. In each case the file stays in the tree as untracked and NOT ignored. It is not in `core.GITIGNORE_ENTRIES`, the hook's `_SESSION_GITIGNORED_PATHS`, this repo's `.gitignore`, or the /clear-boundary disposition. `git check-ignore .prawduct/.pr-body.md` returns nothing. The next `git add -A` commits a PR description into the governed repo. tests/test_hook_session_file_registry.py's docstring (#324) describes exactly this failure: \"A new session file that missed site 3 got committed by accident.\" The registry test cannot catch it because the file is written by skill prose, not by code that a registry lists. This diff even keeps `.prawduct/.pr-reviews/` in this repo's .gitignore for the same leftover-file reason. Instance fix: register the file at all four sites (ignored, and deleted at the boundary), or point the body file outside the repo, e.g. the session scratch directory or `--body-file -` on stdin. Class: grep the skills for Write/draft targets under `.prawduct/` and check each one against GITIGNORE_ENTRIES.", "files": ["plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "plugin/lib/core.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The closure warnings that replace R-2 emit no observable yield, so the proportionality ratchet the plan cites can never judge them", "recommendation": "The plan has two relevant statements. Its governed_by disposition for \"proportionality ratchets both ways\" says the new commands \"name the yield they expect and record each refusal as a fact\". Chunk 01 states the closure results' expected yield as \"the closure results inherit R-2's history\". But `closes-unresolved` and `closes-still-open` are WARNING results. `pr_gate.hygiene_cmd` calls `_record_refusal` only when `exit_code == EXIT_REFUSED`, and only for REFUSE-severity results. So a closure warning is printed to the agent and nothing else: no guard-refusal fact, no ledger event. R-2's history was recorded through `review.pr` events, which nothing writes any more. That history therefore stops here, and the yield the plan says this control inherits is never measured against anything. The norm (nonfunctional-requirements.md: a control must \"emit that yield observably ... a control whose findings are printed and forgotten satisfies the letter and defeats the point\") names exactly this shape. The janitor's Norm Health sweep will have nothing to retire or keep these checks on. Fix: record warning-level hygiene results through the same deduped sink, as a distinct fact or with a severity field, so `evidence list --kind guard-refusal` (or a sibling kind) counts them. Alternatively, state in the plan and the change record that the closure warnings carry no yield and why. Instance: the hygiene command's recording branch.", "files": ["plugin/lib/pr_gate.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Removing learnings-files --for-diff did not check a ruled downstream requirement that names it as the Codex adapter's area-learnings read", "recommendation": "documentation/issues/928-requirements.md has owner-ruled decision D-7 (rows 26 and 27): \"use `learnings-files --for-diff` as the explicit area-learnings read on both hosts and require the Critic to read its result\". That is the planned portable-host (Codex) adapter's mechanism for hosts with no on-read rules loading. The removal ruling of 2026-11-10 and this plan justify outright removal by \"no shipped reader\" and \"the callers ship in the same plugin\". Neither the plan, the change record, nor api-contract.md's REMOVED note mentions #928. So the owner ruled on the removal without being shown that it undoes a mechanism they had already ruled for. A #928 design-stage builder following D-7 will build against a command that no longer exists. .prawduct/artifacts/mcp-knowledge-corpus-design.md \u00a72.2 and route (b) likewise still describe `--for-diff` as a live, automatic Critic consumer. Either record #928/D-7's disposition (super